Setting: $I$ is a 5-element set, $\hat M_{0,I} \simeq \bar M_{0,5}$ is the Deligne–Mumford compactification over Spec Z, and $M^\infty_{0,I}$ is its boundary divisor. Over C, $\bar M_{0,5}$ is the quintic del Pezzo surface.

Previous page (combinatorics). The 10 boundary components $D_T$ are indexed by the 2-subsets $T$, or equivalently by their complementary 'triangles'. The 15 boundary points are indexed by partitions of type (2,2,1), which Grothendieck reads as 'square structures' on 4 of the 5 points. He identifies the 10 components with the faces of the 'icosaèdre gauche', i.e. the hemi-icosahedron (icosahedron modulo the antipodal map: 10 faces, 15 edges, 6 vertices). He identifies the 15 points with its edges. By duality this matches the vertices and edges of the hemi-dodecahedron (10 vertices, 15 edges, 6 pentagonal faces).

This page states a Proposition. $M^\infty_{0,I}$ is a split ('déployée') stable curve over Spec Z. Its dual graph is isomorphic to the 1-skeleton of the hemi-dodecahedron defined by $I$, which is the Petersen graph, once a suitable 'orientation' is chosen.

Details, all correct:
- each component is $\bar M_{0,4} \simeq P^1_Z$;
- each component meets exactly three others, at its three boundary points;
- all branches are unibranch (no self-intersections), and there are no marked points;
- so it is an 'MD-courbe' (Mumford–Deligne curve) with maximal MD-graph: all components rational with exactly 3 special points.

The arithmetic genus is 15 − 10 + 1 = 6. Adjunction gives the same: $\partial^2 = -10 + 30 = 20$, $K\cdot\partial = -10$, so $2g-2 = 10$. Numerically $\partial \equiv -2K$.

Next, Grothendieck remarks on symmetry. The identification with the hemi-dodecahedron is compatible with the action of $G_I^+ = A_5$, which is the automorphism group of the hemi-dodecahedron. It is not compatible with the whole group $G_I^\vee$ (presumably $S_I$). Explanation:
- the Petersen graph has automorphism group $S_5$;
- its 12 pentagons split into two $A_5$-orbits of 6;
- each orbit gives one hemi-dodecahedral face structure, and odd permutations swap the two.
He says this should be understood via the geometry of the hemi-icosahedron/hemi-dodecahedron and the combinatorial 'bi-icosaèdre gauche'. That object presumably carries both structures and is related to the exceptional $S_5 \hookrightarrow S_6$, i.e. $PGL_2(F_5)$ acting on 6 points.

The margin note says $\hat M_{0,I}$ is a genuine scheme, proper and smooth over Z, and that $M_{0,I} \simeq M_{0,\nu}$. The next page passes to the quotient stack by $S_5$: there is a single boundary divisor and a single critical point, by transitivity. So this page serves the description of the 'structure at infinity' of $M_{0,5}$ as a building block for the Teichmüller tower/lego.
